window.__LIVE_NEWS__["2026-11-06"] = {"date":"2026-11-06","articles":[{"id":"n1793950070_0","date":"2026-11-06","source":"BBC","country":"UK","title":"Global tech stocks plunge as US midterms add to AI slowdown fears","summary":"Investors have reacted to possible tougher scrutiny of data centre construction and the AI industry.","body":"BBC News Global tech stocks plunge as US midterms add to AI slowdown fears Image source, Bloomberg via Getty Images By Rachel Clun , Reporting from New York and Osmond Chia , Reporting from Singapore Published 5 November 2026 Updated 1 hour ago Trillions of dollars has been wiped off the stock value of major tech firms in the US, Europe and Asia in a market rout after the US midterm elections sparked concerns of a slowdown in artificial intelligence (AI) development.\n\nShares in firms across the tech sector plummeted following Democratic wins after election campaigns run on slowing AI.\n\nThe tech-heavy Nasdaq composite index closed down by more than 5.5% on Thursday, losing an estimated $2.5tn (£1.9tn) in market value, according to Dow Jones data, while other key US stock indexes also saw big losses in their worst days since 2025.\n\nThe sell-off comes on top of broader concerns about whether the huge amounts being spent on AI will pay off.\n\nIn Asia, tech-heavy indexes fell sharply on Friday morning as firms that make the computer chips and other equipment that power AI saw their shares slide.\n\nThe Nikkei 225 in Japan was 6.3% lower in the afternoon as shares in memory chip maker Kioxia lost almost 11.5% and rival Tokyo Electron slipped by 5.8%.\n\nSouth Korean chipmaker SK Hynix fell by more than 8.8%, while Samsung was down by around 6%.\n\nThe sell-off in Seoul sent the Kospi index plunging more than 8% in morning trading, triggering a so-called circuit breaker, a mechanism that temporarily halts trading to curb panic selling. The Kospi was around 5% lower in the afternoon.\n\nThe Taiwan stock market suffered its biggest weekly decline since 2001, according to LSEG data, with the Taiex falling by 4.5% on Friday.\n\nIt marks a continuation of worries over the level of spending on AI infrastructure, said MarketWatch chips analyst Britney Nguyen.\n\nDemocrats in races across the US campaigned to slow the growth of data centres and AI, and there were dozens of measures on ballots around the country aimed at restricting the development of the hubs crucial for powering the technology.\n\nThey won both the US Senate and House of Representatives in Tuesday&#x27;s midterms, taking control of Congress for the rest of President Donald Trump&#x27;s presidency.\n\nTexas&#x27; Democratic governor-elect Gina Hinojosa campaigned heavily against data centres, pledging a moratorium on new construction in the sector.\n\nLive: What can Democrats do with control of Congress? We answer your US midterms questions\n\nFood, fuel and taxes – can the Democrats actually make life cheaper?\n\nBoebert, billionaires&#x27; tax and other midterms races still to watch\n\nIn a social media post, Trump blamed the slide on his political opponents.\n\nThe Stock Market suffered one of the Worst Days in History based on the results of the Election, he said.\n\nRemember, it wasn&#x27;t Republicans, it was Dumocrats. Do not fear, however, we will soon, one way or the other, be BIGGER AND STRONGER THAN EVER BEFORE. MAKE AMERICA GREAT AGAIN!\n\nLater on Thursday night he appeared to take aim at voters too.\n\nThe Stock Market crashed today because the Dumocrats had a big Tuesday — So sad to see, you only have yourselves to blame! he wrote on Truth Social.\n\nSome Democrats have argued that AI has grown too big without any safeguards or oversight and have promised, with their new power in Congress, to change that.\n\nAmericans don&#x27;t want Big Tech oligarchs wrecking the environment, gutting jobs and threatening humanity&#x27;s future. NATIONAL DATA CENTER MORATORIUM NOW, Democrat Senator Bernie Sanders said in a post on X on Thursday.\n\nShares in companies heavily exposed to AI data centres, including crucial chipmakers and semiconductor manufacturers, fell sharply on Thursday, highlighting investor concerns that had already been growing.\n\nIn European trading on Thursday, shares in chipmaker Infineon fell by more than 8.5% while tech equipment manufacturer ASML dropped by 6.5%.\n\nNvidia shares slid more than 9%, knocking more than $400bn off the firm&#x27;s market value in New York trading.\n\nOther chipmakers also suffered, with AMD and Micron both dropping by more than 17%. Intel shares also dropped, down 10.3%, while memory card maker SanDisk plunged more than 22%.\n\nThe Philadelphia Semiconductor Index, which tracks the performance of 30 of the largest companies involved in semiconductor production, fell by more than 12%.\n\nShares in Elon Musk&#x27;s companies SpaceX and Tesla also plunged, falling 8% and nearly 11% respectively and shaving more than $300bn off the firms&#x27; market value on Thursday.\n\nMusk had spent nearly $450m backing Republican campaigns, including at least $45m in support of Ken Paxton for the Texas senate seat, but Texas was turned blue in the Democratic tsunami on Tuesday night.\n\nIn total, the so-called magnificent seven tech companies, including Apple, Nvidia and Tesla, have shed a trillion dollars in value in a single trading day.\n\nThe stock falls also hit other major US stock indexes. The S P 500 index fell by 4.4% by the end of trading, while the Dow Jones Industrial Average erased gains made on Wednesday to close down more than 3.4%.\n\nThe Nasdaq Composite fall was so large it is now in what market watchers call correction territory, which is where an index or company value falls at least 10% below its recent high.\n\nA backlash over data centres is another threat to the AI juggernaut\n\nPublished 16 September Big, noisy and coming to a town near you - why the data centre backlash is growing\n\nPublished 25 October Susannah Streeter, chief investment strategist at Wealth Club, said the steep sell-off on the Nasdaq and the S P 500 was sending shockwaves into other markets.\n\nFresh panic over the AI trade has hit Wall Street hard, she said.\n\nFears that the vast sums poured into AI infrastructure may not deliver the returns investors have been banking on are now colliding with the prospect of a sharp slowdown in data centre construction.\n\nSome tech leaders have said they were caught off guard by the backlash against data centres in the midterms.\n\nOpenAI president and co-founder Greg Brockman said he believed everyone had been surprised by the reaction to data centres, and more work needed to be done to ensure communities were on board.\n\nA lot of our shift over the course of this year has really been to realise that from San Francisco trying to build infrastructure in Texas, Georgia, and other places, you can&#x27;t just tweet and say: &#x27;This is going to be good.&#x27; You need to be in those communities, Brockman said at the WSJ Tech conference in Napa, California on Wednesday evening.\n\nMeta&#x27;s president Dina Powell McCormick said that the industry needed to put more effort into selling AI as a good idea.\n\nI think we need to double and triple down on telling the story, she told the WSJ conference.\n\nAlongside the immediate impacts of the US mid-term elections, some investors have become concerned that AI shares could be in a bubble that is due a correction .\n\nWith additional reporting from Lily Jamali\n\nThese firms you&#x27;ve never heard of are feeding fears the AI bubble could burst\n\nFood, fuel and taxes – can the Democrats actually make life cheaper?\n\nThe video playlist Watch our pick of standout clips from across the BBC\n\nPrevious Next 1:01 Why are new strikes and protests taking place in France? 00:01:01, play video Why are new strikes and protests taking place in France? 1:06 &#x27;I was professionally ghosted&#x27;: Your worst job interviews. 00:01:06, play video &#x27;I was professionally ghosted&#x27;: Your worst job interviews 1:00 Gordon Ramsay on &#x27;nerve-wracking&#x27; reunion with star chefs. 00:01:00, play video Gordon Ramsay on &#x27;nerve-wracking&#x27; reunion with star chefs 0:43 Love a bedtime snack? You&#x27;re not alone. 00:00:43, play video Love a bedtime snack? You&#x27;re not alone 1:43 Vickrum Digwa&#x27;s sentence increased for Henry Nowak murder. 00:01:43, play video Vickrum Digwa&#x27;s sentence increased for Henry Nowak murder 0:49 Are firework displays too loud for pets and wildlife? 00:00:49, play video Are firework displays too loud for pets and wildlife? 1:24 Married at First Sight UK cancelled after rape allegations. 00:01:24, play video Married at First Sight UK cancelled after rape allegations 0:49 Italian chef fired after refusing to put pineapple on pizza. 00:00:49, play video Italian chef fired after refusing to put pineapple on pizza 0:56 What does the raise in interest rates mean? 00:00:56, play video What does the raise in interest rates mean? 0:50 Are e-bike schemes the &#x27;Wild West&#x27;? 00:00:50, play video Are e-bike schemes the &#x27;Wild West&#x27;? 0:53 What is the polar vortex? 00:00:53, play video What is the polar vortex? 0:37 Why Rice Krispies aren&#x27;t made with rice grains anymore. 00:00:37, play video Why Rice Krispies aren&#x27;t made with rice grains anymore 0:37 Kim K on retaking the bar exam: &#x27;I&#x27;m not a quitter&#x27; 00:00:37, play video Kim K on retaking the bar exam: &#x27;I&#x27;m not a quitter&#x27; 1:27 Christmas ad &#x27;shock&#x27; for Fender&#x27;s old school choir. 00:01:27, play video Christmas ad &#x27;shock&#x27; for Fender&#x27;s old school choir 0:23 &#x27;He knows he was no good&#x27; - Maresca on Donnarumma mistake. 00:00:23, play video &#x27;He knows he was no good&#x27; - Maresca on Donnarumma mistake 1:31 Could Hyrox spell the end for triathlon? 00:01:31, play video Could Hyrox spell the end for triathlon? 1:12 Silver jewellery found to contain toxic chemicals. 00:01:12, play video Silver jewellery found to contain toxic chemicals 0:48 Man sets his heart on keeping rescued badger. 00:00:48, play video Man sets his heart on keeping rescued badger 0:37 What will the weather be like for Bonfire Night? 00:00:37, play video What will the weather be like for Bonfire Night? Top stories Global tech stocks plunge as US midterms add to AI slowdown fears\n\nMet chief warns policing &#x27;heading for failure&#x27; without reform\n\nRussell Brand is being investigated over two new rape complaints, police say\n\nThatcher&#x27;s Right to Buy split my estate. Now its days could be numbered\n\nU2 on Dolly Parton&#x27;s final duet; and the &#x27;grudge match&#x27; behind their new album\n\nA wildfire killed their loved ones in Spain. These British families demand to know why\n\nDid a data centre revolt affect the US mid-term election results?\n\nTempted by snacks before bed? Here&#x27;s why - and what you can do about it\n\nWeekly quiz: How did Rice Krispies lose their snap, crackle and pop?\n\n&#x27;I was asked if I was a crier&#x27;: Your worst job interviews revealed\n\nThe Papers: &#x27;Justice&#x27; and &#x27;Predatory&#x27; Brand faces jail\n\nUS Politics Unspun: Cut through the noise with Anthony Zurcher&#x27;s newsletter\n\nHow could going to bed an hour earlier help your health?\n\nThe story behind Britain&#x27;s love of curry\n\nSam Mullins takes you inside the world of a devious scammer\n\nTrump signs order preventing future presidents from changing Oval Office\n\nGlobal tech stocks plunge as US midterms add to AI slowdown fears\n\nCould the earliest weakening of the polar vortex on record bring UK snow?\n\n&#x27;Justice&#x27; and &#x27;Predatory&#x27; Brand faces jail\n\n&#x27;Misleading&#x27; student loans information left out examples of higher-salary repayments\n\nThatcher&#x27;s Right to Buy split my estate. Now its days could be numbered\n\nAndrew received up to £2,000 per month from sub-letting, watchdog reports\n\nRussell Brand is being investigated over two new rape complaints, police say\n\nHome Office investigating after BBC catches lawyer offering to make fines go away\n\nMet chief warns policing &#x27;heading for failure&#x27; without reform\n\nCopyright © 2026 BBC. The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites. Read about our approach to external linking.","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cmn04705rd8jo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss","source_type":"live","vocabPoints":[{"word":"infrastructure","phonetic":"","definition":"基础设施","example":"It marks a continuation of worries over the level of spending on AI infrastructure, said MarketWatch chips analyst Britney Nguyen.","example2":"The heavy rains badly damaged the region's infrastructure.","en_def":"the basic structure or features of a system or organization；the stock of basic facilities and capital equipment needed for the functioning of a country or area"},{"word":"semiconductor","phonetic":"/ˌsemikənˈdʌktə/","definition":"半导体","example":"Shares in companies heavily exposed to AI data centres, including crucial chipmakers and semiconductor manufacturers, fell sharply on Thursday, highlighting investor concerns that had already been growing.","_bank_example":"Semiconductor exports fell sharply.","example2":"Semiconductor exports fell sharply."},{"word":"intelligence","phonetic":"","definition":"情报；智力","example":"BBC News Global tech stocks plunge as US midterms add to AI slowdown fears Image source, Bloomberg via Getty Images By Rachel Clun , Reporting from New York and Osmond Chia , Reporting from Singapore Published 5 November","example2":"The latest figures show a clear link between the economy and intelligence.","en_def":"the ability to comprehend; to understand and profit from experience；a unit responsible for gathering and interpreting information about an enemy"}],"grammarPoints":[{"pattern":"被动语态 (Passive Voice)","explanation":"be 动词 + 过去分词。新闻大量使用被动语态，因为动作的承受者比执行者更重要。注意 by 短语引出真正的执行者，常被省略。","en":"The passive voice emphasizes the action or its receiver rather than the doer: be + past participle. Used heavily in news when the doer is unknown or less important. The agent can be added with 'by' or omitted entirely.","example":"BBC News Global tech stocks plunge as US midterms add to AI slowdown fears Image source, Bloomberg via Getty Images By Rachel Clun , Reporting from New York and Osmond Chia , Reporting from Singapore Published 5 November","example2":"The bridge was built in 1932 and is still used by thousands of commuters every day."}],"slangPoints":[{"term":"walk back","meaning":"收回（言论）（walk back the remarks）","en":"\"walk back\" — a common English phrase; typical collocation: \"walk back the remarks\".","example":"","example2":"The aide later walked back the controversial statement."}]},{"id":"n1793950070_1","date":"2026-11-06","source":"BBC","country":"UK","title":"White House denies Hegseth and Rubio to leave jobs after Republican election losses","summary":"US media reports there are calls to replace Pete Hegseth, who also faces a possible impeachment vote, while Mark Rubio is rumoured to be eyeing a presidential run.","body":"BBC News White House denies Hegseth and Rubio to leave jobs after Republican election losses Image source, AFP via Getty Images By Sareen Habeshian Published 6 November 2026, 01:31 GMT Updated 5 hours ago The White House has denied rumours that Defence Secretary Pete Hegseth and Secretary of State Marco Rubio might leave their jobs in a post-election shakeup of the Trump administration.\n\nUS media have reported there are calls to replace Hegseth - in particular over his handling of the Iran war, which is being blamed for heavy Republican election losses.\n\nOne outgoing congressional Republican has threatened to force an impeachment vote against Hegseth as early as next week. The defence secretary posted a video statement defending his record and denouncing the effort.\n\nYou see, they attack me. They attack us because we are effective, he said. It&#x27;s not going to work. We will not back down.\n\nVance walks Trump tightrope with scathing election critique blaming Iran war\n\nPublished 1 day ago Meanwhile, Rubio is denying reports he is considering leaving his post ahead of a 2028 presidential run.\n\nWorking for Trump until the end of his term has always been my plan, Rubio told The New York Times in an interview.\n\nI love the work I&#x27;m doing, Rubio said. And plus I always laugh when they say he&#x27;s going to leave his job. I go, &#x27;Which one?&#x27;\n\nThe White House and US State Department also shut down reports that Rubio might be leaving after this week&#x27;s midterm losses, with both calling it fake news .\n\nThe defence secretary, though, faces a more uncertain political future.\n\nTrump and Vice-President JD Vance have both blamed the Iran war for Republican losses that allowed Democrats full control of the US House of Representatives and the Senate.\n\nIn a telephone interview with ABC News, Trump said that he had received calls about Hegseth, but added that he had every intention of keeping him.\n\nLater on Thursday, he told reporters at the White House that Pete Hegseth has done a fantastic job , and went on to list what he sees are some of his achievements, including the arrest of Venezuelan leader Nicolás Maduro in January and the Iran war, now into its ninth month.\n\nRESULTS: How America voted in maps and charts\n\nEXPLAINER: Five reasons why Trump did so badly\n\nZURCHER: Midterm carnage weakens Trump but he still wields power for now\n\nANALYSIS: Vance walks Trump tightrope with scathing election critique blaming Iran war\n\nThe Washington Post reported on Thursday that Trump might be, however, considering ousting General Dan Caine, the chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, or Centcom chief Admiral Brad Cooper. Both played significant roles in offering guidance on the war, which started eight months ago.\n\nMeanwhile, Republican Representative Thomas Massie, a frequent critic of Trump, has said he will push ahead with plans to push for Hegseth&#x27;s impeachment.\n\nHe told news site Politico that he will trigger his resolution to impeach the defence secretary early next week.\n\nAnd on X, he warned that the president should replace Hegseth before a new Democratic-controlled Congress forms in January.\n\nHe should do it while he can still get the replacements confirmed in the Senate, Massie wrote on X.\n\nTo play this video you need to enable JavaScript in your browser. This video can not be played\n\nCould Democrats in Congress end the war in Iran?\n\nHegseth has also been rumoured to be interested in running for an open Tennessee Senate seat.\n\nIn his video statement, Hegseth denounced the sham articles of impeachment effort against him from a disgruntled, defeated congressman, along with all his Democrat allies , and listed ways the department has fulfilled Trump&#x27;s America First agenda under his leadership.\n\nThat includes, he said, reviving military recruitment, rebuilding the defence industrial base, and rooting out diversity initiatives.\n\nOf the Democrats, he said: Their policies don&#x27;t work. So their obsession with political theatre disguised as power drives them to try to destroy their opponents. It&#x27;s all about the politics of personal destruction.\n\nVance says Republicans lost big because of Iran war: &#x27;Everyone knows why we lost&#x27;\n\nThe video playlist Watch our pick of standout clips from across the BBC\n\nPrevious Next 1:01 Why are new strikes and protests taking place in France? 00:01:01, play video Why are new strikes and protests taking place in France? 1:06 &#x27;I was professionally ghosted&#x27;: Your worst job interviews. 00:01:06, play video &#x27;I was professionally ghosted&#x27;: Your worst job interviews 1:00 Gordon Ramsay on &#x27;nerve-wracking&#x27; reunion with star chefs. 00:01:00, play video Gordon Ramsay on &#x27;nerve-wracking&#x27; reunion with star chefs 0:43 Love a bedtime snack? You&#x27;re not alone. 00:00:43, play video Love a bedtime snack? You&#x27;re not alone 1:43 Vickrum Digwa&#x27;s sentence increased for Henry Nowak murder. 00:01:43, play video Vickrum Digwa&#x27;s sentence increased for Henry Nowak murder 0:49 Are firework displays too loud for pets and wildlife? 00:00:49, play video Are firework displays too loud for pets and wildlife? 1:24 Married at First Sight UK cancelled after rape allegations. 00:01:24, play video Married at First Sight UK cancelled after rape allegations 0:49 Italian chef fired after refusing to put pineapple on pizza. 00:00:49, play video Italian chef fired after refusing to put pineapple on pizza 0:56 What does the raise in interest rates mean? 00:00:56, play video What does the raise in interest rates mean? 0:50 Are e-bike schemes the &#x27;Wild West&#x27;? 00:00:50, play video Are e-bike schemes the &#x27;Wild West&#x27;? 0:53 What is the polar vortex? 00:00:53, play video What is the polar vortex? 0:37 Why Rice Krispies aren&#x27;t made with rice grains anymore. 00:00:37, play video Why Rice Krispies aren&#x27;t made with rice grains anymore 0:37 Kim K on retaking the bar exam: &#x27;I&#x27;m not a quitter&#x27; 00:00:37, play video Kim K on retaking the bar exam: &#x27;I&#x27;m not a quitter&#x27; 1:27 Christmas ad &#x27;shock&#x27; for Fender&#x27;s old school choir. 00:01:27, play video Christmas ad &#x27;shock&#x27; for Fender&#x27;s old school choir 0:23 &#x27;He knows he was no good&#x27; - Maresca on Donnarumma mistake. 00:00:23, play video &#x27;He knows he was no good&#x27; - Maresca on Donnarumma mistake 1:31 Could Hyrox spell the end for triathlon? 00:01:31, play video Could Hyrox spell the end for triathlon? 1:12 Silver jewellery found to contain toxic chemicals. 00:01:12, play video Silver jewellery found to contain toxic chemicals 0:48 Man sets his heart on keeping rescued badger. 00:00:48, play video Man sets his heart on keeping rescued badger 0:37 What will the weather be like for Bonfire Night? 00:00:37, play video What will the weather be like for Bonfire Night? Top stories Global tech stocks plunge as US midterms add to AI slowdown fears\n\nMet chief warns policing &#x27;heading for failure&#x27; without reform\n\nRussell Brand is being investigated over two new rape complaints, police say\n\nThatcher&#x27;s Right to Buy split my estate. Now its days could be numbered\n\nU2 on Dolly Parton&#x27;s final duet; and the &#x27;grudge match&#x27; behind their new album\n\nA wildfire killed their loved ones in Spain. These British families demand to know why\n\nDid a data centre revolt affect the US mid-term election results?\n\nTempted by snacks before bed? Here&#x27;s why - and what you can do about it\n\nWeekly quiz: How did Rice Krispies lose their snap, crackle and pop?\n\n&#x27;I was asked if I was a crier&#x27;: Your worst job interviews revealed\n\nThe Papers: &#x27;Justice&#x27; and &#x27;Predatory&#x27; Brand faces jail\n\nUS Politics Unspun: Cut through the noise with Anthony Zurcher&#x27;s newsletter\n\nHow could going to bed an hour earlier help your health?\n\nThe story behind Britain&#x27;s love of curry\n\nSam Mullins takes you inside the world of a devious scammer\n\nTrump signs order preventing future presidents from changing Oval Office\n\nGlobal tech stocks plunge as US midterms add to AI slowdown fears\n\nCould the earliest weakening of the polar vortex on record bring UK snow?\n\n&#x27;Justice&#x27; and &#x27;Predatory&#x27; Brand faces jail\n\n&#x27;Misleading&#x27; student loans information left out examples of higher-salary repayments\n\nThatcher&#x27;s Right to Buy split my estate. Now its days could be numbered\n\nAndrew received up to £2,000 per month from sub-letting, watchdog reports\n\nRussell Brand is being investigated over two new rape complaints, police say\n\nHome Office investigating after BBC catches lawyer offering to make fines go away\n\nMet chief warns policing &#x27;heading for failure&#x27; without reform\n\nCopyright © 2026 BBC. The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites. Read about our approach to external linking.","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cqg7xxg4jdv5o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss","source_type":"live","vocabPoints":[{"word":"administration","phonetic":"","definition":"政府；管理","example":"BBC News White House denies Hegseth and Rubio to leave jobs after Republican election losses Image source, AFP via Getty Images By Sareen Habeshian Published 6 November 2026, 01:31 GMT Updated 5 hours ago The White House","example2":"The latest figures show a clear link between the economy and administration.","en_def":"a method of tending to or managing the affairs of a some group of people (especially the group's business affairs)；the persons (or committees or departments…"},{"word":"congressional","phonetic":"","definition":"a. 会议的, 议会的, 国会的\\ / [法] 代表大会的, 大会的, 议会的","example":"One outgoing congressional Republican has threatened to force an impeachment vote against Hegseth as early as next week.","example2":"Many voters expressed congressional support for the new measure.","en_def":"a. of or relating to congress"},{"word":"professional","phonetic":"","definition":"专业的","example":"1:06 &#x27;I was professionally ghosted&#x27;: Your worst job interviews.","example2":"Experts are concerned about the professional change in policy.","en_def":"a person engaged in one of the learned professions；an athlete who plays for pay"}],"grammarPoints":[{"pattern":"间接引语 / 转述动词 (Reported Speech)","explanation":"said/told/warned/confirmed + that 从句（that 常省略）。新闻标题后正文常用转述动词引述当事人观点，注意从句语序不倒装。","en":"Reported (indirect) speech reports what someone said without using their exact words, typically with a reporting verb (said, told, added) and a tense shift: 'She said she was leaving.'","example":"BBC News White House denies Hegseth and Rubio to leave jobs after Republican election losses Image source, AFP via Getty Images By Sareen Habeshian Published 6 November 2026, 01:31 GMT Updated 5 hours ago The White House","example2":"She told reporters (that) the deal had already been signed."}],"slangPoints":[{"term":"back down","meaning":"让步；退缩（refused to back down 拒绝让步）","en":"\"back down\" — a common English phrase; typical collocation: \"refused to back down\".","example":"We will not back down.","example2":"The government refused to back down over the new tax."}]},{"id":"n1793950070_2","date":"2026-11-06","source":"BBC","country":"UK","title":"Pope begins lengthy trip to Latin America weeks after lung cancer surgery","summary":"Pope Leo, who had part of a lung removed last month, will visit Uruguay, Argentina and Peru.","body":"BBC News Pope begins lengthy trip to Latin America weeks after lung cancer surgery Image source, Reuters Image caption, Pope Leo is travelling to Uruguay, Argentina and Peru\n\nPope Leo XIV is beginning a lengthy trip to Latin America just three weeks after surgery for lung cancer - a tour that includes a much-anticipated homecoming to Peru where he was a priest for many years.\n\nThe Vatican says no extraordinary measures are being taken in relation to the Pope&#x27;s health, describing him as a 71-year-old who has undergone an operation not someone with a permanent medical condition .\n\nIt was Leo himself who revealed last month that doctors had discovered a tumour during a routine scan. The growth was removed along with the lower lobe of his right lung and his medical team then confirmed the cancer had not spread.\n\nAt a briefing in Rome ahead of the trip, which takes in Uruguay and Argentina as well as Peru, a spokeswoman stressed that Pope Leo had fantastic stamina and was extremely enthusiastic about the schedule which he had personally confirmed... with no changes .\n\nThe Vatican&#x27;s message with this trip is, &#x27;that was a short episode and now we&#x27;re back on track&#x27;, Professor Massimo Faggioli of Villanova University told the BBC, pointing out that the Pope has had a busy return to work since his surgery.\n\nWe have seen him in these last few days making speeches, audiences and so on. He seems in great shape. That was important.\n\nImage source, EPA Image caption, On Friday Pope Leo presided over the annual memorial Mass for cardinals and bishops who have died\n\nThe 12-day tour would be demanding for anyone, though. It incorporates multiple long-haul and local flights and covers thousands of miles from the Amazon to the Andes.\n\nThe starting point will be Uruguay, where the number of Catholics has fallen away steeply in recent years and which is getting its first Papal visit in four decades.\n\nLeo then heads for Argentina, homeland of Pope Francis, his predecessor.\n\nImage caption, Father Fernando Laguna said his heart was filled with joy when the Pope&#x27;s visit to Argentina was announced\n\nThis visit is also a homage to Francis, who didn&#x27;t make it to Argentina [as pope], says Father Fernando Laguna, the rector of Santa Maria Addolorata, Argentina&#x27;s national church in Rome.\n\nWhen this visit by Pope Leo was announced, it filled all of our hearts with joy, he told the BBC. I always thought he would go, despite his illness. I don&#x27;t think he ever thought of calling it off!\n\nThe priest is installing a big screen inside the church in Rome to allow parishioners to follow all the key moments of the trip from afar. The building is already decked out with posters declaring Pope Leo is welcome in Argentina. We&#x27;re waiting for you! , one proclaims.\n\nImage caption, &#x27;Welcome Pope Leo&#x27; reads this poster in the Argentinian church in Rome\n\nThis leg of the Pope&#x27;s journey - which includes meetings with heads of state as well as religious events - could be the most politically sensitive. President Javier Milei once called Pope Francis evil on earth and a communist for promoting a doctrine of social justice to help the poor. In turn, Argentina&#x27;s bishops have criticised Milei&#x27;s harsh austerity measures, making sweeping cuts to social spending.\n\nBut Milei has already welcomed Pope Leo, writing in a post on X that Argentina awaits with immense joy . Millions are expected to turn out to greet him.\n\nThe highlight of the trip is undoubtedly saved for last, when Pope Leo reaches Peru - described by the Vatican as a return to his roots.\n\nHe knows these streets, is how a spokeswoman put it.\n\nImage source, EPA Image caption, Worshippers at Chiclayo cathedral in Peru were delighted when their former bishop became Pope in 2025\n\nBorn in the US, the then Robert Prevost spent almost 20 years in Peru, on and off, and even has Peruvian citizenship. That time is seen as fundamental to shaping his papacy.\n\nHis schedule includes a return to the diocese of Chiclayo where he was ordained bishop in 2014 and remained until Francis moved him to the Vatican almost a decade later.\n\nHe will also travel to Cusco, former capital of the Inca Empire, to say Mass more than 3,000m above sea level before heading to Pucallpa in the Amazon.\n\nIn all three countries, the Vatican says the Pope will have meetings with survivors of clerical sexual abuse - an issue which clouded a 2018 visit to Chile and Peru by his predecessor.\n\nThis is how the papacy reconnects with Latin America after Francis, whose visits to the region were not always seamless, is how Professor Faggioli sees it.\n\nHe believes Pope Leo has a strong sense of the unity of the Americas as one continent. Democracies are in trouble there, so there are so many things he can address, he suggests.\n\nThe big question then is the United States. There&#x27;s no question he wants to go. So will he travel when [President Donald] Trump is still in office? I&#x27;m sure they&#x27;re thinking about this.\n\nPope Leo XIV thanks wellwishers in first event after cancer surgery\n\nPope diagnosed with lung cancer but had successful surgery, Vatican says\n\nPope Leo XIV becomes first pontiff to vote in a US election\n\nThe video playlist Watch our pick of standout clips from across the BBC\n\nPrevious Next 1:01 Why are new strikes and protests taking place in France? 00:01:01, play video Why are new strikes and protests taking place in France? 1:06 &#x27;I was professionally ghosted&#x27;: Your worst job interviews. 00:01:06, play video &#x27;I was professionally ghosted&#x27;: Your worst job interviews 1:00 Gordon Ramsay on &#x27;nerve-wracking&#x27; reunion with star chefs. 00:01:00, play video Gordon Ramsay on &#x27;nerve-wracking&#x27; reunion with star chefs 0:43 Love a bedtime snack? You&#x27;re not alone. 00:00:43, play video Love a bedtime snack? You&#x27;re not alone 1:43 Vickrum Digwa&#x27;s sentence increased for Henry Nowak murder. 00:01:43, play video Vickrum Digwa&#x27;s sentence increased for Henry Nowak murder 0:49 Are firework displays too loud for pets and wildlife? 00:00:49, play video Are firework displays too loud for pets and wildlife? 1:24 Married at First Sight UK cancelled after rape allegations. 00:01:24, play video Married at First Sight UK cancelled after rape allegations 0:49 Italian chef fired after refusing to put pineapple on pizza. 00:00:49, play video Italian chef fired after refusing to put pineapple on pizza 0:56 What does the raise in interest rates mean? 00:00:56, play video What does the raise in interest rates mean? 0:50 Are e-bike schemes the &#x27;Wild West&#x27;? 00:00:50, play video Are e-bike schemes the &#x27;Wild West&#x27;? 0:53 What is the polar vortex? 00:00:53, play video What is the polar vortex? 0:37 Why Rice Krispies aren&#x27;t made with rice grains anymore. 00:00:37, play video Why Rice Krispies aren&#x27;t made with rice grains anymore 0:37 Kim K on retaking the bar exam: &#x27;I&#x27;m not a quitter&#x27; 00:00:37, play video Kim K on retaking the bar exam: &#x27;I&#x27;m not a quitter&#x27; 1:27 Christmas ad &#x27;shock&#x27; for Fender&#x27;s old school choir. 00:01:27, play video Christmas ad &#x27;shock&#x27; for Fender&#x27;s old school choir 0:23 &#x27;He knows he was no good&#x27; - Maresca on Donnarumma mistake. 00:00:23, play video &#x27;He knows he was no good&#x27; - Maresca on Donnarumma mistake 1:31 Could Hyrox spell the end for triathlon? 00:01:31, play video Could Hyrox spell the end for triathlon? 1:12 Silver jewellery found to contain toxic chemicals. 00:01:12, play video Silver jewellery found to contain toxic chemicals 0:48 Man sets his heart on keeping rescued badger. 00:00:48, play video Man sets his heart on keeping rescued badger 0:37 What will the weather be like for Bonfire Night? 00:00:37, play video What will the weather be like for Bonfire Night? Top stories Global tech stocks plunge as US midterms add to AI slowdown fears\n\nMet chief warns policing &#x27;heading for failure&#x27; without reform\n\nRussell Brand is being investigated over two new rape complaints, police say\n\nThatcher&#x27;s Right to Buy split my estate. Now its days could be numbered\n\nU2 on Dolly Parton&#x27;s final duet; and the &#x27;grudge match&#x27; behind their new album\n\nA wildfire killed their loved ones in Spain. These British families demand to know why\n\nDid a data centre revolt affect the US mid-term election results?\n\nTempted by snacks before bed? Here&#x27;s why - and what you can do about it\n\nWeekly quiz: How did Rice Krispies lose their snap, crackle and pop?\n\n&#x27;I was asked if I was a crier&#x27;: Your worst job interviews revealed\n\nThe Papers: &#x27;Justice&#x27; and &#x27;Predatory&#x27; Brand faces jail\n\nUS Politics Unspun: Cut through the noise with Anthony Zurcher&#x27;s newsletter\n\nHow could going to bed an hour earlier help your health?\n\nThe story behind Britain&#x27;s love of curry\n\nSam Mullins takes you inside the world of a devious scammer\n\nTrump signs order preventing future presidents from changing Oval Office\n\nGlobal tech stocks plunge as US midterms add to AI slowdown fears\n\n&#x27;Justice&#x27; and &#x27;Predatory&#x27; Brand faces jail\n\n&#x27;Misleading&#x27; student loans information left out examples of higher-salary repayments\n\nThatcher&#x27;s Right to Buy split my estate. Now its days could be numbered\n\nAndrew received up to £2,000 per month from sub-letting, watchdog reports\n\nRussell Brand is being investigated over two new rape complaints, police say\n\nCould the earliest weakening of the polar vortex on record bring UK snow?\n\nMet chief warns policing &#x27;heading for failure&#x27; without reform\n\nHome Office investigating after BBC catches lawyer offering to make fines go away\n\nCopyright © 2026 BBC. The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites. Read about our approach to external linking.","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cqn06j0y61k6o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss","source_type":"live","vocabPoints":[{"word":"extraordinary","phonetic":"","definition":"a. 非常的, 特别的, 非凡的\\ / [经] 非常的, 特别的, 临时的","example":"The Vatican says no extraordinary measures are being taken in relation to the Pope&#x27;s health, describing him as a 71-year-old who has undergone an operation not someone with a permanent medical condition .","example2":"The extraordinary situation has drawn widespread attention.","en_def":"a. beyond what is ordinary or usual; highly unusual or exceptional or remarkable；s. far more than usual or expected"},{"word":"enthusiastic","phonetic":"","definition":"a. 狂热的, 热心的, 热烈的","example":"At a briefing in Rome ahead of the trip, which takes in Uruguay and Argentina as well as Peru, a spokeswoman stressed that Pope Leo had fantastic stamina and was extremely enthusiastic about the schedule which he had per","example2":"The enthusiastic situation has drawn widespread attention.","en_def":"a. having or showing great excitement and interest"},{"word":"fundamental","phonetic":"","definition":"基本的","example":"That time is seen as fundamental to shaping his papacy.","example2":"Many voters expressed fundamental support for the new measure.","en_def":"any factor that could be considered important to the understanding of a particular business；the lowest tone of a harmonic series"}],"grammarPoints":[{"pattern":"定语从句 (Relative Clause)","explanation":"who/which/that 引导的定语从句修饰前面的名词。who 指人、which 指物，that 两者皆可；关系代词在从句中作宾语时可省略。","en":"A relative clause modifies a noun using who (people), which (things), or that (both). When the relative pronoun is the object it can be omitted: 'the law (that) the Senate passed'.","example":"BBC News Pope begins lengthy trip to Latin America weeks after lung cancer surgery Image source, Reuters Image caption, Pope Leo is travelling to Uruguay, Argentina and Peru\n\nPope Leo XIV is beginning a lengthy trip to L","example2":"The scientist who developed the vaccine won the Nobel Prize."}],"slangPoints":[{"term":"uphill battle","meaning":"艰苦的斗争","en":"a very difficult struggle or challenge e.g. \"Reforming the system remains an uphill battle.\"","example":"","example2":"Reforming the system remains an uphill battle."}]},{"id":"n1793950070_3","date":"2026-11-06","source":"BBC","country":"UK","title":"Trump signs order preventing future presidents from changing Oval Office","summary":"The order comes after Trump made a series of big changes to the decor of the Oval Office and tore down the White House East Wing.","body":"BBC News Trump signs order preventing future presidents from changing Oval Office Image source, EPA By Max Matza Published 6 November 2026, 01:20 GMT Updated 2 hours ago US President Donald Trump has signed an executive order that he says will prevent future presidents from making certain changes to the White House Oval Office.\n\nTrump, a former real estate mogul, has drastically altered the look of the president&#x27;s official office during his second term, most notably with adding many gold features.\n\nWe&#x27;re making it a national monument, and it will not be allowed to be touched by anybody, destroyed, ripped down by stupid people, in order to build something in a slightly different shape, Trump told reporters on Thursday.\n\nThe order comes months after Trump tore down the entirety of the White House East Wing in a project that has faced legal challenges.\n\nThe project was also approved by the US Congress. Construction has continued, with plans to turn the space into a new ballroom.\n\nAmerican presidents currently have the power to make some changes to the White House but large projects require the permission of Congress.\n\nThe executive order, which Trump signed on Thursday, declares the Oval Office to be a national monument, along with some of its surrounding rooms.\n\nThe document also says that it does not restrict the President&#x27;s authority to redecorate the monument, select furnishings, or make ordinary operational and security modifications that do not substantially impair the monument&#x27;s historic structure or architectural character .\n\nWe like the Oval Office, and it&#x27;s a symbol, like the White House itself. It&#x27;s a symbol of our country and the greatness of our country, Trump said, adding that world leaders who have visited in recent months have complimented his decorative changes to the room.\n\nTrump said the White House and the Oval Office had been in need of repairs before his changes, calling it badly damaged . He described broken windows, cracked walls and broken stone.\n\nMany of the noticeable changes, though, were cosmetic, including a large gold plaque reading Oval Office outside the space, paving over the Rose Garden and adding a new helicopter pad.\n\nTrump said the order would make it impossible for anybody ever to destroy this incredible space .\n\nHow Trump is remaking the White House in his own image\n\nImage source, Getty Images Image caption, The Oval Office in 2025 during Trump&#x27;s second term in the White House\n\nImage source, Getty Images Image caption, The Oval Office in 2024 during Joe Biden&#x27;s tenure\n\nImage source, Getty Images Image caption, The office in 2015 during Barack Obama&#x27;s time in the White House\n\nThe entire building is already designated as a National Historic Landmark, which is intended to prevent against sudden changes to the building.\n\nAs president, Trump has the power to create national monuments, which are similar to national parks.\n\nTrump&#x27;s order calls on the office to be maintained by the National Park Service in a manner that protects its historic character, architectural integrity, and association with the Presidency .\n\nIt notes the existing protections to the White House, but says nonetheless, the Oval Office merits specific recognition and protective management as a discrete historic structure of national significance .\n\nCongress has carved out an exemption to the National Historic Preservation Act, in order to allow presidents to make needed changes. The current Oval Office has existed since the early 1930s, after its conception by President Franklin D Roosevelt.\n\nGreg Werkheiser, from the law firm Cultural Heritage Partners, which specialises in historic preservation, says that temporary alterations like furniture and decor is completely up to the president&#x27;s discretion .\n\nAs long as they&#x27;re not damaging the permanent art and architecture, they can generally do whatever they want, he told the BBC.\n\nImage source, AFP via Getty Images Image caption, A new gold sign was placed outside the Oval Office\n\nImage source, Getty Images Image caption, A Trump administration staff member seen inside the Oval Office in May\n\nWerkheiser argues that the Trump administration has shown disregard for other historic sites in Washington and, through lawsuits, has attempted to undermine the laws that require preservation.\n\nTrump has undertaken a massive beautification project in Washington and has held three events in the past week to boast of the construction, even as his Republican party was trounced by Democrats in the midterm elections.\n\nWerkheiser called Trump&#x27;s order hypocrisy , noting the various projects he&#x27;s pushed for across Washington - including plans to build a new triumphal arch, which has been temporarily put on hold by a federal judge , and renovating the Kennedy Center.\n\nOn the one hand he&#x27;s talking about the importance of preserving the Oval Office - which is legally meaningless because he can&#x27;t do it - and on the other hand he is absolutely taking an axe to the law that allows us to preserve other historic sites.\n\nIn October, the president suggested in an interview with Time , external magazine that the Oval Office would need to be torn down due to security concerns from the US Secret Service.\n\nI said, &#x27;My ass, you can&#x27;t build a new one. This is a special place,&#x27; Trump said at the time.\n\nDuring the midterm election campaign, Democrats criticised Trump&#x27;s redesigns, with former President Barack Obama saying that his golden tweaks have got the White House looking like Saddam Hussein opened a Cheesecake Factory .\n\nTo play this video you need to enable JavaScript in your browser. This video can not be played\n\nUS midterms are &#x27;last best chance to rein in&#x27; Trump administration, Obama says\n\nTrump&#x27;s massive arch wins approval as protesters threaten to block it\n\nThe video playlist Watch our pick of standout clips from across the BBC\n\nPrevious Next 1:01 Why are new strikes and protests taking place in France? 00:01:01, play video Why are new strikes and protests taking place in France? 1:06 &#x27;I was professionally ghosted&#x27;: Your worst job interviews. 00:01:06, play video &#x27;I was professionally ghosted&#x27;: Your worst job interviews 1:00 Gordon Ramsay on &#x27;nerve-wracking&#x27; reunion with star chefs. 00:01:00, play video Gordon Ramsay on &#x27;nerve-wracking&#x27; reunion with star chefs 0:43 Love a bedtime snack? You&#x27;re not alone. 00:00:43, play video Love a bedtime snack? You&#x27;re not alone 1:43 Vickrum Digwa&#x27;s sentence increased for Henry Nowak murder. 00:01:43, play video Vickrum Digwa&#x27;s sentence increased for Henry Nowak murder 0:49 Are firework displays too loud for pets and wildlife? 00:00:49, play video Are firework displays too loud for pets and wildlife? 1:24 Married at First Sight UK cancelled after rape allegations. 00:01:24, play video Married at First Sight UK cancelled after rape allegations 0:49 Italian chef fired after refusing to put pineapple on pizza. 00:00:49, play video Italian chef fired after refusing to put pineapple on pizza 0:56 What does the raise in interest rates mean? 00:00:56, play video What does the raise in interest rates mean? 0:50 Are e-bike schemes the &#x27;Wild West&#x27;? 00:00:50, play video Are e-bike schemes the &#x27;Wild West&#x27;? 0:53 What is the polar vortex? 00:00:53, play video What is the polar vortex? 0:37 Why Rice Krispies aren&#x27;t made with rice grains anymore. 00:00:37, play video Why Rice Krispies aren&#x27;t made with rice grains anymore 0:37 Kim K on retaking the bar exam: &#x27;I&#x27;m not a quitter&#x27; 00:00:37, play video Kim K on retaking the bar exam: &#x27;I&#x27;m not a quitter&#x27; 1:27 Christmas ad &#x27;shock&#x27; for Fender&#x27;s old school choir. 00:01:27, play video Christmas ad &#x27;shock&#x27; for Fender&#x27;s old school choir 0:23 &#x27;He knows he was no good&#x27; - Maresca on Donnarumma mistake. 00:00:23, play video &#x27;He knows he was no good&#x27; - Maresca on Donnarumma mistake 1:31 Could Hyrox spell the end for triathlon? 00:01:31, play video Could Hyrox spell the end for triathlon? 1:12 Silver jewellery found to contain toxic chemicals. 00:01:12, play video Silver jewellery found to contain toxic chemicals 0:48 Man sets his heart on keeping rescued badger. 00:00:48, play video Man sets his heart on keeping rescued badger 0:37 What will the weather be like for Bonfire Night? 00:00:37, play video What will the weather be like for Bonfire Night? Top stories Global tech stocks plunge as US midterms add to AI slowdown fears\n\nMet chief warns policing &#x27;heading for failure&#x27; without reform\n\nRussell Brand is being investigated over two new rape complaints, police say\n\nThatcher&#x27;s Right to Buy split my estate. Now its days could be numbered\n\nU2 on Dolly Parton&#x27;s final duet; and the &#x27;grudge match&#x27; behind their new album\n\nA wildfire killed their loved ones in Spain. These British families demand to know why\n\nDid a data centre revolt affect the US mid-term election results?\n\nTempted by snacks before bed? Here&#x27;s why - and what you can do about it\n\nWeekly quiz: How did Rice Krispies lose their snap, crackle and pop?\n\n&#x27;I was asked if I was a crier&#x27;: Your worst job interviews revealed\n\nThe Papers: &#x27;Justice&#x27; and &#x27;Predatory&#x27; Brand faces jail\n\nUS Politics Unspun: Cut through the noise with Anthony Zurcher&#x27;s newsletter\n\nHow could going to bed an hour earlier help your health?\n\nThe story behind Britain&#x27;s love of curry\n\nSam Mullins takes you inside the world of a devious scammer\n\nTrump signs order preventing future presidents from changing Oval Office\n\nGlobal tech stocks plunge as US midterms add to AI slowdown fears\n\nCould the earliest weakening of the polar vortex on record bring UK snow?\n\n&#x27;Justice&#x27; and &#x27;Predatory&#x27; Brand faces jail\n\n&#x27;Misleading&#x27; student loans information left out examples of higher-salary repayments\n\nThatcher&#x27;s Right to Buy split my estate. Now its days could be numbered\n\nAndrew received up to £2,000 per month from sub-letting, watchdog reports\n\nRussell Brand is being investigated over two new rape complaints, police say\n\nHome Office investigating after BBC catches lawyer offering to make fines go away\n\nMet chief warns policing &#x27;heading for failure&#x27; without reform\n\nCopyright © 2026 BBC. The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites. Read about our approach to external linking.","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cmy0eeg101djo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss","source_type":"live","vocabPoints":[{"word":"construction","phonetic":"","definition":"建设","example":"Construction has continued, with plans to turn the space into a new ballroom.","example2":"The report highlighted construction as a key factor in the decision.","en_def":"the act of constructing something；a group of words that form a constituent of a sentence and are considered as a single unit"},{"word":"significance","phonetic":"","definition":"意义","example":"It notes the existing protections to the White House, but says nonetheless, the Oval Office merits specific recognition and protective management as a discrete historic structure of national significance .","example2":"The report highlighted significance as a key factor in the decision.","en_def":"the quality of being significant；a meaning that is not expressly stated but can be inferred"},{"word":"architecture","phonetic":"","definition":"n. 建筑学, 建筑式样\\ / [计] 体系结构","example":"As long as they&#x27;re not damaging the permanent art and architecture, they can generally do whatever they want, he told the BBC.","example2":"The debate over architecture is unlikely to end soon.","en_def":"an architectural product or work；the discipline dealing with the principles of design and construction and ornamentation of fine buildings"}],"grammarPoints":[{"pattern":"现在完成时 (Present Perfect)","explanation":"have/has + 过去分词。表示过去发生、对现在有影响的动作。新闻中常用来强调\"已经发生\"的结果，常与 already、just、so far 连用。","en":"The present perfect (have/has + past participle) links a past action to the present moment. Used when the exact time is not given or the result still matters now: 'Prices have fallen sharply.'","example":"BBC News Trump signs order preventing future presidents from changing Oval Office Image source, EPA By Max Matza Published 6 November 2026, 01:20 GMT Updated 2 hours ago US President Donald Trump has signed an executive ","example2":"Prices have fallen sharply since January, and analysts expect the trend to continue."}],"slangPoints":[{"term":"in high demand","meaning":"需求旺盛","en":"much wanted; desired by many people e.g. \"Skilled engineers are in high demand.\"","example":"","example2":"Skilled engineers are in high demand."}]},{"id":"n1793950070_4","date":"2026-11-06","source":"BBC","country":"UK","title":"California governor commutes sentences for state's 548 death row inmates","summary":"California has historically had the largest population of people on death row in the US.","body":"BBC News California governor commutes sentences for state&#x27;s 548 death row inmates Image source, Reuters Image caption, California Governor Gavin Newsom&#x27;s term in office comes to an end in January\n\nCalifornia Governor Gavin Newsom has commuted the death sentences of all 548 people on death row in the state, changing them instead to life in prison.\n\nWe can now let these offenders die in obscurity while we forever remember and honour the victims, he said in a letter.\n\nThe decision to spare death row inmates is largely symbolic in a state that has not executed an inmate since 2006, though experts say it could save millions of dollars in court costs each year. Only 13 inmates have been executed since voters reinstated the death penalty in 1978.\n\nThe decision comes as Newsom, the Democratic leader of the most populous US state, has been discussed as a possible 2028 contender for president.\n\nNewsom, who is term limited, is winding down his final months in office. Fellow Democrat Xavier Becerra is projected to take over after the midterm elections on Tuesday.\n\nNewsom explained in his letter that he is exercising his constitutional clemency authority to commute the sentences to life without the possibility of parole.\n\nAfter nearly 30 years in public office, I have come to understand the limits of what government can justly accomplish, Newsom said. I take this action because I firmly believe that state-sanctioned killing is incompatible with California&#x27;s values, undermines the foundations of our legal system and diminishes each of us .\n\nCalifornia has historically had the largest population of death row inmates. Newsom had previously ordered a halt to the death penalty in the state in 2019.\n\nTo play this video you need to enable JavaScript in your browser. This video can not be played\n\nAmericans&#x27; views on death penalty after failed execution in Tennessee\n\nDescribing his decision as a difficult but righteous action , Newsom said that although many Californians feel that those who commit brutal crimes deserve to die, he has concluded that the state cannot morally kill them .\n\nHe said that by every measure, California&#x27;s system for administering the death penalty is a failure , and that it has not and cannot be fairly applied, resulting in the conviction of innocent people.\n\nLife without the possibility of parole is the most severe punishment he can lawfully impose and that the state can ethically enforce, he said.\n\nHe acknowledged that his clemency action will anger and hurt some Californians and said that it will always weigh heavily on me .\n\nAs governor, I alone bear responsibility for this decision and its impact, just as I alone would bear responsibility for each execution, he wrote.\n\nFor this reason, I am compelled to follow my conscience and, as authorised by our Constitution, take this action for the welfare of the State of California.\n\nBecause individual states have the power to prosecute crimes and order executions, much of the power lies with state governors.\n\nBernard E Harcourt, a law and civil liberties professor at Columbia University, told the BBC that it is not unprecedented for a potential presidential candidate to come out as an abolitionist in the run up to elections - despite the overall popularity of the death penalty in the US.\n\nHe noted that François Mitterrand did precisely that in his presidential campaign in France in 1981, taking the higher ethical ground and was rewarded by the public for that .\n\nI detect a very similar ethical positioning in Governor Newsom&#x27;s statements about the commutations. And here too, it may well be something that the voters respect, he said.\n\nImage source, Getty Images Image caption, San Quentin State Prison&#x27;s used to house California&#x27;s death row adjustment centre\n\nNewsom&#x27;s decision comes after the high-profile botched execution of a woman in Tennessee in late September and while executions have been on the rise in the US. There are three executions scheduled for Thursday in Indiana, Florida and Alabama.\n\nAt least four of seven judges on the California Supreme Court will have to sign off on the commutations, CalMatters reported.\n\nIt is unclear how Newsom&#x27;s decision will affect condemned inmates like Keith Doolin, 53, who was sentenced to death in 1996 for murdering two sex workers, Inez Espinoza and Peggy Tucker, and shooting four others. He maintains his innocence.\n\nDoolin lived on death row at San Quentin for decades, until he was moved to another prison in 2025 when the governor announced the state would reform the prison for all but those on death row .\n\nDoolin&#x27;s mother, Donna Larsen, told the BBC that the news of Newsom&#x27;s commutations - which includes Doolin - brought mixed emotions.\n\nAt least the execution chamber will not be reconstructed, and no more killing by the people of the state of California, she said.\n\nAnthony D Romero, the executive director of the American Civil Liberties Union, applauded Newsom&#x27;s action.\n\nThese commutations reflect what justice demands: an end to a racist, error-prone, and inhumane punishment that has burdened California for far too long, Romero said.\n\nBy taking this step, Gov. Newsom has affirmed that the death penalty has no place in California and he showed real courage by confronting racialised injustice head-on.\n\nNathan Hochman, the top prosecutor in Los Angeles County, said that while Newsom has the power to commute sentences, only state legislature or voters can eliminate the death penalty.\n\nCalifornians have been asked on two separate ballot initiatives to abolish the death penalty and both times they have chosen to keep it for the most serious crimes, Hochman said in a statement.\n\nTo play this video you need to enable JavaScript in your browser. This video can not be played\n\nHow widely used is the death penalty in the US?\n\nOn the federal level, President Donald Trump, a long-time supporter of the death penalty, has described capital punishment as an essential tool for deterring and punishing those who would commit the most heinous crimes and acts of lethal violence against American citizens .\n\nIn 2020, during his first term, he ended a nearly two-decade moratorium on the federal death penalty, by ordering 13 executions over a six-month period.\n\nWhile the moratorium returned under President Biden, on the first day of Trump&#x27;s second term in 2025, he signed an executive order that re-instated the death penalty.\n\nNo federal executions have been carried out since he signed it, however. In the US, both the federal government and local states authorities can order executions.\n\nThe death penalty is legal in 27 states, but has been abolished in 23 states and Washington DC, according to the Death Penalty Information Center, a national non-profit.\n\nCalifornia, which primarily uses lethal injection for executions, used to house male death row prisoners in San Quentin State Prison north of San Francisco, and female inmates in the Central California Women&#x27;s Facility in the Central Valley.\n\nBut a 2016 ballot measure allowed for their transfer to other facilities that provide a necessary level of security, according to the California Department of Corrections and Rehabilitation.\n\nWhy the death penalty is on the rise in US\n\nUS inmate Christa Pike says she woke up &#x27;confused and in pain&#x27; after failed execution\n\nWhat happened in Christa Pike&#x27;s failed US execution, and how did she survive?\n\nThe video playlist Watch our pick of standout clips from across the BBC\n\nPrevious Next 1:01 Why are new strikes and protests taking place in France? 00:01:01, play video Why are new strikes and protests taking place in France? 1:06 &#x27;I was professionally ghosted&#x27;: Your worst job interviews. 00:01:06, play video &#x27;I was professionally ghosted&#x27;: Your worst job interviews 1:00 Gordon Ramsay on &#x27;nerve-wracking&#x27; reunion with star chefs. 00:01:00, play video Gordon Ramsay on &#x27;nerve-wracking&#x27; reunion with star chefs 0:43 Love a bedtime snack? You&#x27;re not alone. 00:00:43, play video Love a bedtime snack? You&#x27;re not alone 1:43 Vickrum Digwa&#x27;s sentence increased for Henry Nowak murder. 00:01:43, play video Vickrum Digwa&#x27;s sentence increased for Henry Nowak murder 0:49 Are firework displays too loud for pets and wildlife? 00:00:49, play video Are firework displays too loud for pets and wildlife? 1:24 Married at First Sight UK cancelled after rape allegations. 00:01:24, play video Married at First Sight UK cancelled after rape allegations 0:49 Italian chef fired after refusing to put pineapple on pizza. 00:00:49, play video Italian chef fired after refusing to put pineapple on pizza 0:56 What does the raise in interest rates mean? 00:00:56, play video What does the raise in interest rates mean? 0:50 Are e-bike schemes the &#x27;Wild West&#x27;? 00:00:50, play video Are e-bike schemes the &#x27;Wild West&#x27;? 0:53 What is the polar vortex? 00:00:53, play video What is the polar vortex? 0:37 Why Rice Krispies aren&#x27;t made with rice grains anymore. 00:00:37, play video Why Rice Krispies aren&#x27;t made with rice grains anymore 0:37 Kim K on retaking the bar exam: &#x27;I&#x27;m not a quitter&#x27; 00:00:37, play video Kim K on retaking the bar exam: &#x27;I&#x27;m not a quitter&#x27; 1:27 Christmas ad &#x27;shock&#x27; for Fender&#x27;s old school choir. 00:01:27, play video Christmas ad &#x27;shock&#x27; for Fender&#x27;s old school choir 0:23 &#x27;He knows he was no good&#x27; - Maresca on Donnarumma mistake. 00:00:23, play video &#x27;He knows he was no good&#x27; - Maresca on Donnarumma mistake 1:31 Could Hyrox spell the end for triathlon? 00:01:31, play video Could Hyrox spell the end for triathlon? 1:12 Silver jewellery found to contain toxic chemicals. 00:01:12, play video Silver jewellery found to contain toxic chemicals 0:48 Man sets his heart on keeping rescued badger. 00:00:48, play video Man sets his heart on keeping rescued badger 0:37 What will the weather be like for Bonfire Night? 00:00:37, play video What will the weather be like for Bonfire Night? Top stories Global tech stocks plunge as US midterms add to AI slowdown fears\n\nMet chief warns policing &#x27;heading for failure&#x27; without reform\n\nRussell Brand is being investigated over two new rape complaints, police say\n\nThatcher&#x27;s Right to Buy split my estate. Now its days could be numbered\n\nU2 on Dolly Parton&#x27;s final duet; and the &#x27;grudge match&#x27; behind their new album\n\nA wildfire killed their loved ones in Spain. These British families demand to know why\n\nDid a data centre revolt affect the US mid-term election results?\n\nTempted by snacks before bed? Here&#x27;s why - and what you can do about it\n\nWeekly quiz: How did Rice Krispies lose their snap, crackle and pop?\n\n&#x27;I was asked if I was a crier&#x27;: Your worst job interviews revealed\n\nThe Papers: &#x27;Justice&#x27; and &#x27;Predatory&#x27; Brand faces jail\n\nUS Politics Unspun: Cut through the noise with Anthony Zurcher&#x27;s newsletter\n\nHow could going to bed an hour earlier help your health?\n\nThe story behind Britain&#x27;s love of curry\n\nSam Mullins takes you inside the world of a devious scammer\n\nTrump signs order preventing future presidents from changing Oval Office\n\nGlobal tech stocks plunge as US midterms add to AI slowdown fears\n\n&#x27;Justice&#x27; and &#x27;Predatory&#x27; Brand faces jail\n\n&#x27;Misleading&#x27; student loans information left out examples of higher-salary repayments\n\nThatcher&#x27;s Right to Buy split my estate. Now its days could be numbered\n\nAndrew received up to £2,000 per month from sub-letting, watchdog reports\n\nRussell Brand is being investigated over two new rape complaints, police say\n\nCould the earliest weakening of the polar vortex on record bring UK snow?\n\nMet chief warns policing &#x27;heading for failure&#x27; without reform\n\nHome Office investigating after BBC catches lawyer offering to make fines go away\n\nCopyright © 2026 BBC. The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites. Read about our approach to external linking.","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cqrkyyd2j8mvo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss","source_type":"live","vocabPoints":[{"word":"responsibility","phonetic":"","definition":"责任","example":"As governor, I alone bear responsibility for this decision and its impact, just as I alone would bear responsibility for each execution, he wrote.","example2":"The incident has once again put responsibility under the spotlight.","en_def":"a form of trustworthiness; the trait of being answerable to someone for something or being responsible for one's conduct"},{"word":"constitution","phonetic":"","definition":"n. 构成, 宪法, 体格\\ / [医] 体质; 结构, 组织","example":"For this reason, I am compelled to follow my conscience and, as authorised by our Constitution, take this action for the welfare of the State of California.","example2":"Campaigners welcomed the move, saying it reflects growing concern about constitution.","en_def":"the act of forming or establishing something；the way in which someone or something is composed"},{"word":"possibility","phonetic":"","definition":"可能性","example":"Newsom explained in his letter that he is exercising his constitutional clemency authority to commute the sentences to life without the possibility of parole.","example2":"Many voters expressed possibility support for the new measure.","en_def":"a future prospect or potential；capability of existing or happening or being true"}],"grammarPoints":[{"pattern":"条件句 (Conditional)","explanation":"if 引导的条件从句。真实条件用一般现在时（if X happens, Y will…）；新闻中也常见虚拟条件表假设。","en":"Conditionals express conditions and results. The first conditional (if + present, will) is used for real future possibilities. Common in policy and economic news.","example":"&#x27;I was asked if I was a crier&#x27;: Your worst job interviews revealed\n\nThe Papers: &#x27;Justice&#x27; and &#x27;Predatory&#x27; Brand faces jail\n\nUS Politics Unspun: Cut through the noise with Anthony Zurcher&#x2","example2":"If the strike continues into next week, hundreds of flights will be cancelled."}],"slangPoints":[{"term":"all but","meaning":"几乎；差不多（all but collapsed）","en":"almost; very nearly e.g. \"The insurgency has all but collapsed in recent months.\"","example":"Doolin lived on death row at San Quentin for decades, until he was moved to another prison in 2025 when the governor announced the state would reform the prison for all but those on death row .","example2":"The insurgency has all but collapsed in recent months."}]}]};
